---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Joey Hershkop — Software Engineer & Builder" 
  description="Joey Hershkop — Toronto software engineer (McGill '25). Founding engineer at Thea, and the builder of ParkKing, a Toronto curb parking app."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Joey Hershkop",
    url: "https://joeyhershkop.com",
    jobTitle: "Founding Engineer",
    worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Thea" },
    alumniOf: { "@type": "CollegeOrUniversity", name: "McGill University" },
    address: { "@type": "PostalAddress", addressLocality: "Toronto", addressRegion: "ON", addressCountry: "CA" },
    sameAs: [
      "https://github.com/JoeHershkie",
      "https://linkedin.com/in/jhershkop",
      "https://joeyhershkop.substack.com",
      "https://joeyhershkop.bandcamp.com"
    ]
  })} />
  <div class="intro-section">
    <p class="lead-text">
      I'm Joey — a computer engineer (McGill '25) in Toronto who builds software end-to-end and thinks across disciplines.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm the founding engineer at Thea, an early-stage startup that places private residential mortgage deals in Ontario. Before that I spent a year building a production B2B SaaS platform: PostgreSQL schemas, APIs, React clients, billing, access control, and the tests around them.
    </p>

    <p>
      On my own time I build <a href="/work/parkking/" class="text-link">ParkKing</a>, a free iPhone app that shows whether you can park at any curb in Toronto, for the time you choose. It started as a data problem, turning the City's parking bylaws, written as legal prose, into a map, and it is now live on the App Store with an Android version in the works.
    </p>

    <p>
      Outside of engineering, I'm driven by broad curiosity. I spend a lot of time reading psychology, neuroscience, statistics, and philosophy, composing music, and diving into problems that don't fit neatly into a single discipline.
    </p>

    <p>
      Explore my <a href="/work/" class="text-link">selected work</a>, download my <a href="/resume.pdf" class="text-link">resume (PDF)</a>, listen to <a href="/music/" class="text-link">music</a>, check out upcoming <a href="/blog/" class="text-link">writing</a>, or <a href="/contact/" class="text-link">get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="featured-work-heading">
    <h2 id="featured-work-heading">Featured Projects</h2>
    
    <div class="work-list">
      <article class="work-item">
        <h3><a href="/work/parkking/" class="text-link">ParkKing — Toronto Curb Parking, Live on the App Store</a></h3>
        <p>
          A free iPhone app that colour-codes every curb in Toronto for the time you'll be there. I built the Python pipeline that turns the City's bylaws and ticket data into curb-level rules, and the native app that evaluates them offline, instantly.
        </p>
        <ul class="work-tags" aria-label="Technologies used">
          <li>Python</li>
          <li>GeoPandas</li>
          <li>Shapely</li>
          <li>SwiftUI</li>
          <li>MapKit</li>
          <li>SQLite</li>
          <li>Kotlin</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="work-links">
          <a href="/work/parkking/" aria-label="Read the ParkKing case study">Read Case Study &rarr;</a>
          <a href="https://getparkking.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">getparkking.com &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="work-item">
        <h3>HoverBot — Vision-Based Navigation System</h3>
        <p>
          A multithreaded computer-vision pipeline in Python and OpenCV that sustains 30 fps with &le; 100 ms image-to-actuation latency and &plusmn; 3 cm mean arrival error across 30 physical waypoints.
        </p>
        <ul class="work-tags" aria-label="Technologies used">
          <li>Python</li>
          <li>OpenCV</li>
          <li>NumPy</li>
          <li>Robotics</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
